the sanitiser is a rule and not a habit, and it has the tests it never had

§3.1's falsification half. The operator's framing is the reason this commit exists:
**leticl has two tests here and this head had the fix and none** — so nothing proved the
sanitising would notice if it stopped working. The vocabulary is the store's own (44
`tool_result` rows carry an escape, 20 a mode string), not a list invented for a test:
an SGR pair and a reset, `ESC[8m`, `ESC[2J`, `?1002`/`?1006`/`?1049`/`?2004`/`?2026`, an
OSC window title, C1 CSI and ST, and DEL.

## The test found six holes on its first run, which is the whole argument for it

Each one is a surface this head renders and does not own, and every one of them was
reaching the terminal raw:

1. **A tool-progress note** — `card::Phase::Running`'s tail, pushed straight through at
   `card.rs:425`. This is the surface the gate's own sentence rides.
2. **The queued echo** — `queued_lines`, while `user_block` (the settled row the same
   words become) was already guarded. Two callers, one of them forgotten.
3. **The slash listing** — the echo and every row of `/notes`, `/job`, `/gate`.
4. **Four panes** — a job's command line and state, a subagent's prompt, role and state,
   a todo's content, the picker's session title, the header's title.
5. **And a second defect entirely:** five sites emitted `sgr::REVERSE`/`sgr::RESET`
   **unconditionally**, so a `--replay` or a pipe to a file carried `ESC[7m` for every
   highlighted row. That is `Palette::None`'s contract (no sequences at all) broken in
   the one configuration whose whole point is clean text.
6. **The structural one.** `crates/ui` — the crate that draws every card — had **zero**
   occurrences of `without_control`. The guard lived in the head, so the crate below it
   could not be covered by construction.

## The fix is a rule in two places rather than a habit in twenty

- **`letibot_ui::text`** is new: the two functions, moved out of the head. One definition
  in the crate both sides already depend on is what stops the next renderer being the
  leaky one.
- **`Palette::paint` sanitises its own text argument.** A palette's argument is *text* by
  definition — the head's own sequences are the ones `open`/`close` supply — so a control
  byte in it is somebody else's bytes painted as though they were words. One line here
  covers every painted string in both crates; the call sites that were already guarding
  are now belt and braces.
- **The renderers that own their input guard it themselves**: `card::render`/`header`,
  `user_block`, `queued_lines`, the slash listing, and the pane fields that are pushed
  unpainted. A caller cannot forget what the callee does.

## Why the colourless half is the sharper assertion

With `color: false` the head emits **no escapes at all**, so "not one ESC in these rows"
is a precise statement about the *text* — SGR pair included. With colour on, an SGR pair
in model prose is **indistinguishable in the byte stream from this head's own red**, so
asserting its absence there would be either false or meaningless; the coloured half
therefore names only the sequences this head never writes (an OSC, the `?nnnn` modes
other than `?25h`/`?25l`, `ESC[8m`, a screen clear, C1, DEL), where finding one is proof
regardless of what the palette was doing.

## The test also caught two mistakes in itself, which is worth recording

- Its first pane loop set four panes at once and labelled all four "the jobs pane", while
  `slash_out` won the screen every time — **a label describing the loop rather than the
  frame**, which is the same defect the test exists to catch. It now sets one pane at a
  time and asserts the pane was actually drawn.
- The earlier `render_one` helper was hardcoded to no colour; it is now `item_rows(colour,
  item)` because the two halves need both.

## Tests

- `app::tests::no_escape_from_content_this_head_did_not_author_reaches_the_terminal` —
  nine transcript surfaces (model prose, a **fence body**, reasoning, the operator's own
  paste, a system row, a tool payload, a failure reason, the raw `ctrl-x` markup, both
  sides of a diff **and its path**) plus a live turn (streamed prose, reasoning, a
  progress note, a queued prompt) plus four panes plus the header's renamed title.
- `letibot_ui::text`'s own two tests, moved with the functions, pinning the pair that is
  the whole point: the document form keeps its lines, and the single-line form still
  flattens.
- `content_this_head_did_not_write_cannot_reconfigure_the_terminal` (from `e0a99b9`) is
  unchanged and still passes — it is the narrower earlier sweep.

```
cargo test -p letibot-tui   317 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored
cargo test -p letibot-ui    105 passed, 0 failed
```

**Not verified, and said rather than glossed:** `rustfmt --check` cannot run — it writes a
temp file and `bash` is closed for that direction by the circuit breaker, which only the
operator lifts. No card was observed on a real terminal: the assertions are on the rows
and the bytes, which is where a regression would show and is what a test can hold.

## Not in this commit

**`note` / `free` on a question answer** (§1.7's remainder) and the rest of §6 — clicks
reaching five panes, SGR mouse motion, the tail-origin sign for the two overlays, which
is the next item.
